<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Partabgarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1425 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Pratapgarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sisodia Rajput state in southern Rajasthan, an offshoot of Mewar, known for its distinctive Thewa jewellery of gold worked onto glass.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The rulers descended from a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/udaipur-state">Mewar</a> that settled in the Kanthal region; tradition dates their rule to the early 15th century. The early capital was Deolia, and Maharawat Pratap Singh founded the new capital of Pratapgarh in 1698.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Partabgarh was a hilly state with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> population. It came under Mughal and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> domination, paying tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkar</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indore-state">Indore State</a>, and signed a treaty with the British in 1818. Local goldsmiths developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thewa">Thewa</a>, a technique of fusing gold filigree onto coloured glass.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Partabgarh joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a> in 1948. Pratapgarh became a separate district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 2008.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/partabgarh-state.jpg" alt="Partabgarh State" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers, revectorised by Cypp0847 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Partabgarh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1425 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pratapgarh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pratap Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malvi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pratapgarh, Kanthal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1686&amp;focus=partabgarh-state" aria-label="Partabgarh State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6671 1292 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Partabgarh State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6991 1548L7364 1601M6991 1548L7364 1602M6991 1548L6996 1446"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6991 1548h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6991 1548h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pratapgarh. <a href="/atlas?year=1686&amp;focus=partabgarh-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
